test interpret
test run
target scry32

; A `br_table` in a non-entry block whose *default* arm is a cold block. A
; sentinel is returned instead of trapping so the wrong path is observable:
; 999 = the default arm.
;
; The essential ingredients, found by ablation:
;   - the `br_table` in a block that is not the entry block,
;   - `cold` on the *default* arm (a cold table arm with a hot default
;     passes, as does the same switch placed in the entry block).
; A single table arm suffices; the loop back edge, the call, and the checked
; add of the originating scenario are all incidental.
;
; The guarded-against failure mode is that every input takes the default arm:
; with the cold default sunk to the end of the layout, an arm's target lands
; right after the table block, and the arm's conditional jump to it must not
; be elided as a fall-through -- that would lose its role of cancelling the
; pending default jump, which would then always trigger. Instead every table
; block is padded with a dead `nop` so an arm's jump to the next block stays
; encodable (see `pad_multi_issue_blocks`).
function %m(i32 uext) -> i32 uext {
block0(v0: i32):
    v1 = iconst.i32 1
    v2 = iadd.i32 v0, v1
    jump block1(v2)

block1(v3: i32):
    br_table v3, block3, [block2]

block2:
    v4 = iconst.i32 7
    return v4

block3 cold:
    v5 = iconst.i32 999
    return v5
}
; run: %m(-1) == 7
; run: %m(0) == 999
; run: %m(1) == 999
